{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, when Pharaoh calls you.</b> One might suspect that he calls you in order to select from among you men for his wars; therefore, when he asks you, 'What is your occupation?' you shall say, 'Your servants have been men of livestock,' and you shall not say to him explicitly that you are shepherds, for shepherds are an abomination to the Egyptians, and it is not the way of propriety to speak a thing that is abominable in the king's eyes, and it is shameful to mention explicitly before him a thing that goes against his religion. For if this were not so, why did Joseph not give this reason on his own behalf, when he said that they are shepherds and his reason was 'for every shepherd is an abomination to the Egyptians,' etc.? Rather, on the contrary — this is giving a reason for why he did not command them to say that they are shepherds. Indeed the king will understand on his own: since you are men of livestock, surely you are all shepherds. And so that he will not ask you to change the trade of shepherding for the craft of something else, therefore you shall say 'from our youth even until now' — we have not grown accustomed to any other craft but this. And so that he will not say that from your youth you chose this craft to contradict the belief of those who believe in the sign of Aries, therefore you shall say 'also our fathers' — from of old, before this belief spread in the world. But Joseph wanted to say explicitly to Pharaoh that they are shepherds, for thus he said at the outset that he wished to say before him, 'and the men are shepherds' — for to him it is possible to say so explicitly in order that he may distance you from himself. But for you it is not possible to say explicitly before him something that would cause distancing; rather, he will understand this from the implication of your words." "And in the end the arrangement was reversed: Joseph did not say before Pharaoh that they are shepherds, while his brothers said explicitly, 'Your servants are shepherds.' For he saw that Pharaoh did not send after them to summon them and was not concerned lest he take from them warriors for his wars; therefore he did not need to mention that they are shepherds. And they said explicitly, 'Your servants are shepherds,' in order that he might distance them from his border, as stated. For thus Joseph stipulated: one of them should say explicitly that they are shepherds, and one should say merely 'men of livestock' — either he himself or his brothers. But if so, what difference does it make that Joseph told his brothers what he intended to speak before Pharaoh? Rather, thus Joseph said to his brothers: 'If I speak explicitly that you are shepherds, then it is enough for you to say \"men of livestock,\" for it is not the way of propriety to mention \"shepherds\" twice, since it is against his religion; and if I do not say \"shepherds\" explicitly, then you shall say it explicitly.' And so it was in the end, and through this all the difficulties were resolved."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:49.939348527Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}